      "output": "Welcome to the team. Understanding how deadline budgets, retries, cancellation, and idempotency interact is critical for building reliable distributed systems. These four concepts form a cohesive strategy to manage failure, latency, and consistency across network boundaries.\n\nA **deadline budget** defines the maximum time allowed for a request to complete, including all internal processing and downstream calls. When a client sets a deadline, it propagates through the service chain. If a service exceeds its allocated time, it must stop work and return an error. This prevents \"zombie\" requests that consume resources long after the client has given up.\n\n**Retries** are attempts to resend a request after a transient failure, such as a network timeout or a 503 Service Unavailable response. However, retries must respect the original deadline budget. If the initial attempt consumes 80% of the budget, the retry must fit within the remaining 20%. Blindly retrying without checking the remaining time can lead to wasted effort and increased load on downstream services.\n\n**Cancellation** is the mechanism to stop work when a deadline expires or a client disconnects. When a deadline is exceeded, the system triggers cancellation signals down the call chain. Services must listen for these signals and abort their operations promptly",
